::GEO_U04_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have two pairs of congruent corresponding angles. Which criterion justifies similarity? {
=A. AA similarity#AA similarity applies because two angle pairs determine the third angle pair, so the triangles have the same shape.
~B. SSS congruence#Congruence requires equal side lengths, but the evidence given is angle equality for similarity.
~C. HL congruence#HL applies only to right-triangle congruence, not a two-angle similarity argument.
~D. CPCTC#CPCTC is used after congruence is proven, not to justify triangle similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>The sides of one triangle are 4, 6, 8. The corresponding sides of another are 6, 9, 12. What similarity criterion is supported? {
~A. ASA congruence#ASA proves congruence when side and angle measures match exactly, not proportional side lengths.
=B. SSS similarity#The ratios <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>, and <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub> are all 1.5, so all side pairs are proportional.
~C. SAS congruence#SAS congruence needs equal included sides, but these sides are proportional, not equal.
~D. No similarity conclusion#A similarity conclusion is supported because all corresponding side ratios are equal.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have one congruent included angle and the adjacent side ratios are both 2. Which criterion justifies similarity? {
~A. AA similarity#AA similarity requires two angle pairs; this evidence includes one angle and two side ratios.
~B. SSS similarity#SSS similarity requires all three side ratios, but only two adjacent side ratios are given.
=C. SAS similarity#SAS similarity uses a congruent included angle and proportional adjacent side lengths.
~D. Reflection symmetry#Reflection symmetry maps a figure onto itself and does not by itself justify triangle similarity here.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A dilation maps &triangle;ABC to a triangle congruent to &triangle;DEF. What conclusion is justified? {
~A. &triangle;ABC is not related to &triangle;DEF#The transformation sequence gives evidence connecting the triangles.
~B. Only one side is proportional#A dilation scales all lengths by the same factor, not just one side.
~C. The triangles must be identical in size#A dilation can change size, so similarity is supported even when congruence is not.
=D. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;DEF#A dilation followed by congruence-preserving motion supports similarity between the original and final triangles.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is the strongest similarity justification? {
=A. Two angle pairs are congruent, so the triangles are similar by AA.#This names the evidence and the correct similarity criterion.
~B. The triangles look like the same shape.#Appearance is not enough; a mathematical criterion must be named.
~C. One pair of sides is proportional.#One side ratio alone is not enough to prove triangle similarity.
~D. The triangles are in the same diagram.#Location in a diagram does not justify similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;A &cong; &angle;D and &angle;B &cong; &angle;E, which triangle similarity statement matches the correspondence? {
~A. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;EFD#This order maps A to E instead of D, so the correspondence is incorrect.
=B. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;DEF#The order matches A to D and B to E, so C corresponds to F.
~C. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;FED#This reverses several corresponding vertices.
~D. &triangle;ABC &sim; &triangle;DFE#This maps B to F instead of E.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two side ratios are equal, but the included angles are not congruent. Can SAS similarity be used? {
~A. Yes, equal side ratios alone are enough for SAS.#Side ratios alone do not satisfy SAS because the included angle condition is missing.
~B. Yes, any angle can be used.#The angle must be the included angle between the proportional sides.
=C. No, the included angles must also be congruent.#SAS similarity requires proportional adjacent sides and a congruent included angle.
~D. No, because similar triangles cannot use side ratios.#Similarity often uses side ratios; the issue here is the missing included angle evidence.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence is enough for SSS similarity? {
~A. One pair of sides is congruent.#One side pair does not establish similarity.
~B. One angle pair is congruent.#One angle pair alone is not enough for triangle similarity.
~C. Two triangles share a vertex.#Sharing a vertex gives location information, not similarity evidence.
=D. All three pairs of corresponding sides are proportional.#SSS similarity is based on all three corresponding side ratios being equal.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle is enlarged by a scale factor of 3. Which statement is true about the image triangle? {
=A. It is similar to the original triangle.#A dilation preserves angle measures and scales side lengths proportionally, so the image is similar.
~B. It is always congruent to the original triangle.#A scale factor of 3 changes side lengths, so congruence is not guaranteed.
~C. It has different angle measures.#Dilations preserve angle measures.
~D. It has no proportional side lengths.#Dilations create proportional side lengths using the same scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which criterion uses two side ratios and the angle between those sides? {
~A. AA similarity#AA is based on two angle pairs, not side ratios.
=B. SAS similarity#SAS similarity uses two proportional side pairs and the congruent included angle.
~C. SSS similarity#SSS uses all three side ratios, not an included angle.
~D. CPCTC#CPCTC is not a triangle similarity criterion.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have two pairs of congruent corresponding angles. Which criterion justifies similarity? {
~A. SSS congruence#Congruence requires equal side lengths, but the evidence given is angle equality for similarity.
